WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.760
The A to Z of isms... socialism.

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:06.280
Socialism as an idea
has been around since antiquity -

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:09.120
with some arguing that
the teachings of Jesus Christ

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.440
and the Prophet Muhammad,
were themselves socialist.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:16.560
Socialism is, in essence, the idea
that when a business does well

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.960
the workers, not the owners,
ought to receive the rewards.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:22.400
That holds whether the
business in question is a farm,

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:26.320
a high street bank, an online
retailer or a social media company.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:30.320
But how to achieve that?
That’s where it gets complicated.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:32.640
For some socialists,
the best way to do that

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:35.120
is for the workers
to become the owners.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:37.120
That might mean
through a co-operative,

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.640
where a firm’s employees collectively
own it, and all share the profits.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:44.360
Or it could be the government
steps in and nationalises it,

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:45.960
as Prime Minister Clement Attlee did

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:49.120
when he nationalised the “commanding
heights” of the British economy,

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.680
from the railways to the post offices
in the late 1940s and early 50s.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:57.200
For others, who owns a business
isn’t that important.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:00:59.680
Through taxing the rich
and using the proceeds

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.040
to pay for schools,
hospitals and transport,

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:04.840
and through setting minimum levels
for how much workers should be paid,

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:07.560
the benefits of a business can be
redistributed from the owners

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:10.080
to those who put in
the hard labour to make it happen.

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.440
Or, as the French Socialist
Ségolène Royal put it:

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:20.160
Socialism really only acquired a
concrete theory in the 19th Century,

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:24.160
when the philosopher Karl Marx laid
out what he saw as the driving force

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:26.320
behind the revolutions
that were sweeping Europe.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:29.480
That helped to inspire the formation
of a number of political parties

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:32.400
throughout the continent,
including the British Labour Party.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:35.400
After that, socialists split
into two different groups -

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:38.720
social democrats, who wanted to
moderate the global economic system,

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:42.160
so it worked in a broadly similar way
but benefited workers not bosses,

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:45.280
and communists, who wanted
to replace the global order

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:48.200
with an entirely new way
of running the economy.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:50.240
Some say the problem
with socialist ideals

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:52.800
in human development
is based on the profit motive -

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:56.600
we do things to benefit ourselves
and our families, not other people.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.400
By taking the benefits of enterprise
away from the bosses,

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:03.200
we disincentivise everyone from
working and we all get poorer,

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:04.800
workers and bosses alike.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:06.920
Some socialists
thought this criticism

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:08.760
had more than a grain of truth.

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:10.680
They wanted a new form of socialism

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:12.800
that benefited workers
and bosses alike.

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:14.520
They called this The Third Way -

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:17.280
between traditional,
supposedly unfashionable socialism

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:21.080
and the unrestrained markets favoured
by right-wing parties in the 1980s.

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:24.000
“Socialism”, at least as far as
Karl Marx understood it,

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:25.680
was dead and gone... forever.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:27.640
At least until
the global financial crisis,

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:31.640
when the costs fell on workers
and most bosses escaped unscathed.

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:34.800
In the wake of the financial crisis,
voters got very angry,

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:37.880
and supporters of the Third Way
took a lot of the electoral pain.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:40.120
Many voters, particularly the young,

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:43.120
started to think that perhaps
a return to old-style socialism,

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:45.600
not social democracy,
was the way forward.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:47.480
Socialism is back -

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:49.800
and its political supporters
have gone back to its roots,

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:52.320
advocating four-day weeks,
a basic income,

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:54.760
and government control
over much of the economy.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:58.160
Ten years after Marx’s version
of socialism was declared dead,

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:00.480
there are whispers of a resurrection.

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:08.720
Thanks for watching. 
